{{Culture of Balisca}}

'''Hammams in Balisca''' ({{Langx|bl|hammames}}; {{Langx|mxi|حمامات|Ḥamāmāt}}) are [[W:Public bathhouse|public bathhouses]] following a tradition that combines the [[W:Moorish architecture|Andalusi]] three-room architectural plan with a [[W:Public bathing|bathing culture]] analogous to that of the [[W:Onsen|''onsen'']] tradition. The hammam is one of the most widespread and distinctive civic institutions in [[Balisca]], with an estimated 4,200 operating facilities serving approximately 133 million weekly users as of 2025.

The Baliscan hammam tradition originated in the [[Volisanian charter system|Volisanian charter]] period, when [[Andalus|Andalusi]] merchants and [[Mozbayáns|Mozbayán]] artisans built the first public bathhouses within the segregated ''[[W:Factory (trading post)|fundaco]]'' quarters of the colonial cities. Following the collapse of Volisanian colonial authority in 1736, hammams expanded rapidly beyond the fundaco boundaries, becoming the first integrated public institution in Baliscan urban life. In the post-[[Baliscan Revolution|revolutionary]] period, the [[Cooperative movement in Balisca|cooperative movement]] adopted the hammam as a model for communal infrastructure, and the tradition was further reinforced by the [[Desconexión]] movement's back-to-land settlements. The contemporary Baliscan hammam is simultaneously a functioning bathhouse, a cooperative institution, an Andalusi cultural heritage site, and a major tourism draw.

== History ==

=== Early fundaco hammams (1564–1736) ===

The first hammams in Balisca were built within the ''[[fundaco]]'' system established by the [[Volisanian charter system|Volisanian charters]] of the mid-16th century. Under the charter, [[Andalus|Andalusi]] merchants and [[Mozbayáns|Mozbayán]] artisans were restricted to designated trading posts and reductions, where they constructed public bathhouses following the Andalusi architectural tradition — the three-room axial sequence of ''[[sala fría]]'' (frigidarium), ''[[sala tibia]]'' (tepidarium), and ''[[sala caliente]]'' (caldarium), with [[hypocaust]] heating, domed ceilings pierced by star-shaped skylights, and marble slab surfaces for the scrub.

The [[hammam of Zocodover]], built in 1611 on the artificial island of [[Zocodover]] in [[Salto Bay]], is the oldest continuously operating public bathhouse in the [[Baliscan Levante|Levante]] and one of the finest surviving examples of Andalusi colonial architecture in Balisca. The Zocodover hammam's original hypocaust system, fed by a natural [[W:Hot spring|hot spring]] beneath the island, remains functional after four centuries of continuous use.

Within the fundaco, the hammam served ritualistic, hygienic, and social functions simultaneously. It was the one public space in the chartered quarter that the Volisanian ''[[batlle]]'' did not supervise — an interior, communal space where the Andalusi community governed alone. Gender-segregated facilities or hours were maintained from the beginning, with separate hammams for men and women, each serving as parallel social infrastructure for their respective communities.

=== Expansion and proliferation ===
{{Further|Volisanya Boba|}}
The collapse of Volisanian colonial authority in 1736 as a result of the [[Volisanian Civil War]] — effectively ended the charter system's enforcement capacity. The fundaco walls did not come down; they simply ceased to mean anything, especially as there was no bailiff to enforce them. The Andalusi community, already a major commercial pillar of the [[Alpujarra|Alpujarran]] coast, built new hammams outside the old boundaries as they moved into new areas previously prohibited to them. Within a generation, most areas within the Alpujarra possessed a hammam.

The expansion of the hammam can be considered the first act of social integration in proto-Baliscan urban life. The Andalusi bathhouses were amongst the best-built, best-maintained public infrastructure in the colonial cities, and every community wanted to use them. [[Volisanian Baliscans|Volisanian]], [[Catharism|Catharist]], Muslim, and [[Kafto-Baliscans|Kafto-Baliscan]] bodies shared the same steam — not because of a policy or a decree, but because the plumbing was superior and the door was open. Integration happened through the infrastructure before it happened through the law. By the time the [[Proclamation of Alcabaza]] abolished slavery in 1809, the hammam had been integrated for two generations.

The architectural vocabulary of the expansion-era hammams shifted from the strict [[W:Islamic geometric patterns|Islamic geometric tradition]] toward a syncretic decorative program: Volisanian [[W:Ceramic tile|tilework]], [[Maroons|Maroon]] textile patterns, and indigenous botanical motifs appeared alongside the traditional star-shaped skylights and arabesque borders. The three-room plan was maintained, but the social meaning of the space had fundamentally changed — the hammam was no longer a Andalusi-Islamic institution that others used; it was a Baliscan institution with Andalusi roots.

=== Contemporary hammams (1918–present) ===

{{See also|Baliscan Revolution|Baliscan post-revolution social policy|Desconexión}}

The [[Baliscan Revolution]] (1918–1922) formalized what the hammam already was. The cooperative system adopted the bathhouse as a model for communal infrastructure — maintained by the housing cooperative, open to all members, funded through the cooperative's budget rather than user fees. The [[Plan de Vivienda Popular]] (1922–1929) included hammam construction as a standard component of the new ''[[barrios revolucionarios]]'', and the [[Baliscan Recording Cooperative|cooperative model]] ensured that the quality of the bathhouse was determined by the community's commitment to its maintenance, not by the user's ability to pay.

The economic, political and social crises of the [[Special Period]] reinforced the relevance of hammans, and when [[Desconexión|adherents of the "back-to-land movement"]] moved to rural communes and erected housing and infrastructure, the hammam was among the first facilities they constructed — using the same hypocaust principles with local materials and cooperative labor. Rural hammams, typically smaller and simpler than their urban counterparts, adopted a vernacular tradition of exposed stone, timber-framed skylights, and wood-fired heating that contrasted with the marble and ceramic of the older hammams.

The contemporary Baliscan hammam operates within a tension between four functions: it is a functioning bathhouse serving weekly users, a cooperative institution maintained by community labor, a [[Andalusi cultural heritage]] site protected by preservation law, and a domestic and international tourism attraction marketed by tourism agencies, [[W:Guidebooks|guidebooks]] and travel bloggers. The management of these overlapping claims — particularly the tension between tourist access and community use — is a persistent subject of local political debate.

== Architecture ==
{{Further|Architecture of Balisca|Andalusi architecture}}
[[File:Ali Gholi Agha hammam, Isfahan, Iran.jpg|thumb|The interior of [[Hammam Barbashtur]] ([[Arrabád]]) after hours (2023)]]
The Baliscan hammam follows the Andalusi three-room axial plan, adapted over four centuries of use:

* The '''[[sala fría]]''' (frigidarium) — the cold room, containing the cold plunge pool and the drying area. This is the room of re-entry: the shock of cold water after the heat, the drying with the ''[[W:Peshtemal|toallita]]'', the return to clothing and the world outside.
* The '''[[sala tibia]]''' (tepidarium) — the warm room, containing the soaking pools at body temperature, the marble slab for the ''[[frotano]]'' scrub, and the washing stations. This is the social room: the place where conversation happens, where cooperative assemblies informally convene, where the community governs itself in steam.
* The '''[[sala caliente]]''' (caldarium) — the hot room, containing the steam chamber and the hot soaking pool. This is the room of silence: the heat demands it, the steam requires it, and the cultural norm enforces it.

The three rooms are arranged axially, with the changing room and entrance at one end and the caldarium at the other. The sequence — cold to warm to hot, or more precisely, entry → wash → warm soak → scrub → hot soak → steam → cold plunge → dry → dress — is the structural logic of the building and the ritual logic of the bath. The sequence is non-negotiable; skipping rooms or reversing the order is considered a breach of etiquette and, more practically, a shock to the circulatory system.

The domed ceilings of the hot and warm rooms are pierced by star-shaped or circular skylights (''[[W:Oculus (architecture)|oculi]]'') that admit shafts of light into the steam-laden air. The floors are marble, sloped toward drainage channels. The walls are finished in [[W:Islamic geometric patterns|geometric tilework]] in the colonial and expansion-era hammams, or in exposed stone and timber in the post-Special Period vernacular tradition. The hypocaust system — originally fed by wood-fired furnaces, now typically supplemented by geothermal and solar thermal sources — heats the floors and walls by radiant heat, producing the characteristic ambient warmth that distinguishes the hammam from the dry heat of a [[W:Sauna|sauna]].

== Etiquette ==
{{Further|Hammam#Bathing practices and services|Onsen#Etiquette}}
Baliscan hammam etiquette synthesizes the Islamic ritual tradition with Illypnian [[W:Sauna|sauna]] and [[Sanese people|Sanese]] ''[[W:Onsen|onsen]]'' bathing cultures, and the communalist social contract. The core principle is that the hammam is a shared space maintained by shared discipline — the etiquette exists to protect the water, the steam, and the community that uses them.

=== Nudity and the toallita ===

Patrons are fully nude in the bathing areas. The small towel — the ''[[W:Peshtemal|toallita]]'' — serves as a drying cloth and may provide a modicum of modesty when walking between rooms, held loosely in front of the body. The ''toallita'' is never worn into the soaking pools or the steam chamber; it is placed folded on top of the head or set on the marble ledge beside the water. Immersing or dipping the ''toallita'' in the bath water is considered a major violation of hammam etiquette — it is considered unclean, it contaminates the shared water, and it marks the offender as someone who does not understand the most basic rule of the space. Children learn this rule before they learn the sequence.

The full-nudity norm is the most egalitarian aspect of the hammam. With no clothing at all — not even the traditional ''[[W:Fouta towel|futa]]'' [[W:Fouta towel|wrap]] wrap of the Islamic hammam — there is nothing to distinguish the cooperative director from the dockworker, the merchant from the farmer. The body is the great equalizer, and the Baliscan hammam takes this further than either its Islamic or its East Fosian parent traditions because it combines the communalist ethic with a cultural comfort with nudity that is neither exhibitionist nor puritanical. Bodies are simply bodies; the hammam is where you take care of yours.

=== Sequence of rooms ===

The three-room progression is observed without exception:

# '''Changing room''' — disrobe, store clothing in locker, take the ''toallita''.
# '''Washing stations''' — sit on the low stool, rinse, scrub. '''Wash before soak.''' ''[[frotano|Frotanos]]'' (matching the gender of the visitor) are available for a professional scrub/massage on a marble slab with the use of soap and vigorous rubbing. A ''Frotano's'' scrub and massage in a hammam involves vigorous muscle kneading and [[W:Cracking joints|joint cracking]]. The soaking pool receives you clean.
# '''Sala tibia''' — the warm soaking pool. Social conversation is appropriate here. The ''toallita'' is on the head or the ledge.
# '''Sala caliente''' — the hot soaking pool and steam chamber. Near-silence is the norm. The ''toallita'' is on the head.
# '''Sala fría''' — the cold plunge and subsequent shock and drying-off prior to re-entry into the world.

It is common practice to say "''[[buen baño]]''" on the way out. Skipping rooms, reversing the order, or shortcutting directly from the changing room to the hot room is considered a breach of both etiquette and basic circulatory hygiene.

=== The frotano ===

{{Main|Frotano}}
[[File:Masaje na Hammam painting.webp|thumb|''Masaje na Hammam'' by [[Kwamé Adéyemi]] (1899), depicting a frontano attending to a patron]]
The ''[[frotano]]'' (from {{langx|bl|frotar}}, "to rub" + {{langx|bl|-ano}}, the productive Baliscano demonym suffix) is the professional bathhouse attendant who performs the scrub. The frontano is dressed in a ''[[W:Fouta|futa]]'' [[W:Fouta|cloth]]. The patron lies nude on the marble slab in the warm room; the ''frotano'' works with the ''[[kese]]'' (the rough exfoliating mitt, from {{langx|mxi|كيسة|kīsa}}, "sack"). The patron does not direct or supervise the frotano — the scrub is a surrender of bodily control to a trained professional.

In the cooperative era, the ''frotano'' is a cooperative worker, paid by the cooperative rather than tipped by the user. The abolition of tipping was one of the earliest reforms of the revolutionary period, intended to eliminate the class gradient that had developed in the colonial hammam — where wealthy patrons could expect superior service or "favors" by paying more. The cooperative ''frotano'' gives the same scrub to every body on the slab.

=== Prohibited conduct ===

The following are considered violations of hammam etiquette:

* '''Immersing the ''toallita'' in the bath water''' — the cardinal violation.
* '''Soaping or washing in the soaking pool''' — the pool is for soaking, not for washing. Patrons must wash at the stations before entering.
* '''Photography or phone use''' — phones are forbidden at the door, enforced by the cooperative. The hammam is a space where the body is present and the digital world is excluded.
* '''Staring''' — the nudity norm depends on mutual non-attention. Looking is natural; staring is considered a violation.
* '''Conversation in the sala caliente''' — the hot room should be silent. Brief responses to direct questions are acceptable; sustained conversation is not.
* '''Running''' — the marble is wet and the risk of injury is real.
* '''Wearing perfume or scented products''' — these interfere with the steam and the shared atmosphere.
* '''Shaving at the washing stations while others are waiting''' — the stations are communal; prolonged occupation is inconsiderate.

=== The greeting ===

'''''[[Buen baño]]''''' — "good bath." The standard greeting of the hammam, spoken on arrival, in passing, and on departure. It is not formal or religious; it is the acknowledgment that you and this stranger are sharing the same space, and that this is a good thing.

== Gender identity and inclusion ==

Baliscan hammams operate in three configurations: ''men's'', ''women's'', and ''integrated'' (gender-neutral). All three follow identical architectural plans, etiquette, and cooperative governance. The choice of configuration is determined by the cooperative that operates the facility, reflecting the community it serves rather than any confederal or regional mandate.

Gender-segregated facilities — the historical norm — remain the most common configuration, particularly in older neighborhoods and in communities with strong Andalusi or Kafto-Baliscan cultural traditions. The segregation is architectural, not social: both men's and women's hammams serve the same civic functions — informal political negotiation, cooperative assembly, community governance, and social bonding. The women's hammam is not a domestic sphere extended into public life; it is a parallel public square, as politically and socially consequential as the men's.

Integrated hammams have become increasingly common since the mid-20th century, particularly in new cooperative-built facilities, university districts, and the urban centers of the [[Silicon Coast]]. Their emergence was not the product of a political movement or a policy directive but of demographic and cultural shift — younger cooperatives, more mixed neighborhoods, and communities that simply did not see gender segregation as necessary to the hammam's social function. In integrated facilities, the same etiquette applies: the nudity norm, the sequence, the silence in the hot room, the ''[[buen baño]]''. The cultural norm of non-attention — looking is natural, staring is a violation — extends naturally to gender diversity in the shared space.

=== Transgender inclusion ===
{{See also|Transgender rights in Balisca}}
Transgender patrons use the facility that corresponds to their [[W:Gender identity|gender identity]]. In segregated facilities, [[W:Trans women|trans women]] use the women's hammam and trans men use the men's. This is not a subject of sustained political controversy in Balisca; it follows from the same cultural logic that governs the hammam's approach to the body more broadly — bodies are bodies, the hammam is where you take care of yours, and the cooperative does not police the gender of the bodies on the marble slab.

The cooperative system's approach to transgender inclusion was not the product of a single policy decision but of accumulated practice: cooperatives that admitted transgender patrons found that the existing etiquette — the nudity norm, the non-attention norm, the ''[[frotano]]'''s professional indifference to the body being scrubbed — already contained the framework for inclusion. The ''frotano'' scrubs the body on the slab; the body's gender history is irrelevant to the scrub. 

In integrated facilities, the question does not arise. In segregated facilities, the cooperative's policy is typically stated in the entrance signage: patrons use the facility matching their gender identity. Disputes are rare and are handled through the cooperative's internal governance process rather than through legal or regulatory action.

